 ↓
Post Processing
 ↓
Final Output



🧠 Big Picture (Mental Model)
Text
 ↓
Tokenizer
 ↓
POS Tagger
 ↓
Rule Engine
 ↓
Dictionary Engine (Trie + Fuzzy)
 ↓
Entity Resolver
 ↓
Post Processing
 ↓
Final Output
🔍 Step-by-Step Flow (Your Code Execution)
1️⃣ core.js → extract()

This is your entry point:

extract(text) {
  tokens = tokenizer.tokenize(text)
  taggedTokens = posTagger.tag(tokens)

  ruleEntities = ruleEngine.run(taggedTokens)
  dictEntities = dictionaryEngine.lookup(taggedTokens)

  allEntities = [...ruleEntities, ...dictEntities]

  resolved = resolver.resolve(allEntities)

  final = postProcessors(resolved)

  return final
}
🧩 2️⃣ Tokenizer (tokenizer.js)
Input:
"I live in New York and work at Gooogle"
Output (simplified):
[
  { value: "I", normalized: "i", index: 0 },
  { value: "live", normalized: "live", index: 1 },
  { value: "in", normalized: "in", index: 2 },
  { value: "New", normalized: "new", index: 3 },
  { value: "York", normalized: "york", index: 4 },
  { value: "and", normalized: "and", index: 5 },
  { value: "work", normalized: "work", index: 6 },
  { value: "at", normalized: "at", index: 7 },
  { value: "Gooogle", normalized: "gooogle", index: 8 }
]

👉 Important:

Keeps positions
Keeps original + normalized
🧠 3️⃣ POS Tagger (posTagger.js)

Adds grammar awareness:

[
  { value: "I", pos: "PRON" },
  { value: "live", pos: "NOUN" },
  { value: "in", pos: "PREP" },
  { value: "New", pos: "PROPN" },
  { value: "York", pos: "PROPN" },
  ...
  { value: "Gooogle", pos: "PROPN" }
]

👉 Now rules can say:

only match PROPN → names/places
⚙️ 4️⃣ Rule Engine (ruleEngine.js)

👉 Applies your manual rules

Example rule:

"work at X" → company
What it does:
Loops tokens LEFT → RIGHT
Applies each rule
Produces entities
Output (example):
[
  {
    label: "company",
    value: "Gooogle",
    ruleId: "company_after_at",
    confidence: 0.9
  }
]

👉 Important:

This is raw extraction
Can be wrong (like Gooogle)
📚 5️⃣ Dictionary Engine (dictionaryEngine.js)

This is your smart layer

5.1 Exact Match (Trie)

Checks:

New York → FOUND ✅

👉 Output:

{
  label: "city",
  value: "New York",
  ruleId: "dict_city_exact",
  confidence: 0.95
}
5.2 Fuzzy Match

Now for:

Gooogle → Google
Steps:
normalize:
gooogle → gooogle
normalize repeats:
gooogle → google
compare with dictionary:
google vs google → distance = 0

👉 Output:

{
  label: "company",
  value: "Google",   // corrected
  ruleId: "dict_company_fuzzy",
  confidence: 0.75
}
🧠 Combined Entities

Now system merges:

[
  { city: "New York", confidence: 0.95 },
  { company: "Gooogle", confidence: 0.9 },   // from rule
  { company: "Google", confidence: 0.75 }    // from fuzzy
]
⚖️ 6️⃣ Entity Resolver (entityResolver.js)

This is the judge ⚖️

Conflict: company
Gooogle (0.9)
Google (0.75)

👉 According to rules:

Higher confidence → wins

So:

company = "Gooogle" ❌ (problem)